{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794393581,"nonce":"WZsg7d0KXLa2leUUOogttyKYFic=","message":"The single-thread driver is in, approved by the user the same day.\n\n`hxn_future::stepped` takes work as a closure returning `Poll`, wraps it as a `krio_core::Task`, and runs its first step before returning. Without threads the rest is queued on a thread-local `krio_runtime::RoundRobin`; `Future::wait` runs rounds for a 4 ms slice and returns, and `pump` runs one round. With threads the rest still runs on a thread of its own, yielding between steps.\n\n`spawn` is unchanged: a blocking closure is one step, so without threads it still runs before returning.\n\nTested by 5 new unit tests that drive the queue on a native thread, and by the counter fixture's `sumLater` on Ash native, Ash wasm with fibers, stock HashLink, hxcpp, and hxcpp through Emscripten. On both wasm builds the future is pending when the call returns and completes while awaited, and 20 at once interleave.\n\nkrio is a git dependency pinned to ecf740f8de98965d3c0f238b4d22636318821836.\n\nStill open from the assessment: the native pool (`krio-parallel` needs a `Park` that blocks), a Rust `Future` adapter, and calling Haxe closures from a task stepped on the Haxe thread. Also new: work queued without threads advances only while the host waits or pumps, so a future nobody waits on never finishes there.","files":null}]}